msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20880
#, no-wrap
msgid "Showing the Sectioning Structure of a File"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20881
#, no-wrap
msgid "Showing the sectioning structure of a file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20882
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sectioning structure of a file, showing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20883
#, no-wrap
msgid "Structure of a file, showing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20884
#, no-wrap
msgid "Outline of file structure, showing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20885
#, no-wrap
msgid "Contents-like outline of file structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20886
#, no-wrap
msgid "File sectioning structure, showing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20887
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo file sectioning structure, showing"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20898
msgid ""
"You can show the sectioning structure of a Texinfo file by using the "
"@kbd{C-c C-s} command (@code{texinfo-show-structure}).  This command lists "
"the lines that begin with the @@-commands for @code{@@chapter}, "
"@code{@@section}, and the like.  It constructs what amounts to a table of "
"contents.  These lines are displayed in another buffer called the "
"@samp{*Occur*} buffer.  In that buffer, you can position the cursor over one "
"of the lines and use the @kbd{C-c C-c} command "
"(@code{occur-mode-goto-occurrence}), to jump to the corresponding spot in "
"the Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20900
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-show-structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20901
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-s"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20902
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-show-structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20905
msgid ""
"Show the @code{@@chapter}, @code{@@section}, and such lines of a Texinfo "
"file."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20906
#, no-wrap
msgid "occur-mode-goto-occurrence"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20907
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-c"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20908
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x occur-mode-goto-occurrence"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20911
msgid ""
"Go to the line in the Texinfo file corresponding to the line under the "
"cursor in the @file{*Occur*} buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20919
msgid ""
"If you call @code{texinfo-show-structure} with a prefix argument by typing "
"@w{@kbd{C-u C-c C-s}}, it will list not only those lines with the "
"@@-commands for @code{@@chapter}, @code{@@section}, and the like, but also "
"the @code{@@node} lines.  You can use @code{texinfo-show-structure} with a "
"prefix argument to check whether the `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers "
"of an @code{@@node} line are correct."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20928
msgid ""
"Often, when you are working on a manual, you will be interested only in the "
"structure of the current chapter.  In this case, you can mark off the region "
"of the buffer that you are interested in by using the @kbd{C-x n n} "
"(@code{narrow-to-region}) command and @code{texinfo-show-structure} will "
"work on only that region.  To see the whole buffer again, use @w{@kbd{C-x n "
"w}} (@code{widen}).  (@xref{Narrowing, , , emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}, for "
"more information about the narrowing commands.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20929
#, no-wrap
msgid "page-delimiter"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20930
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page delimiter in Texinfo mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20939
msgid ""
"In addition to providing the @code{texinfo-show-structure} command, Texinfo "
"mode sets the value of the page delimiter variable to match the "
"chapter-level @@-commands.  This enables you to use the @kbd{C-x ]} "
"(@code{forward-page}) and @kbd{C-x [} (@code{backward-page})  commands to "
"move forward and backward by chapter, and to use the @kbd{C-x n p} "
"(@code{narrow-to-page}) command to narrow to a chapter.  @xref{Pages, , , "
"emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}, for more information about the page commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20941 texinfo/texinfo.texi:20943
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using @code{texinfo-show-structure}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20943
msgid "@nodedescription How to use @code{texinfo-show-structure}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20945
#, no-wrap
msgid "Showing the structure of a file"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20950
msgid ""
"It is not always easy to keep track of the nodes, chapters, sections, and "
"subsections of a Texinfo file.  This is especially true if you are revising "
"or adding to a Texinfo file that someone else has written."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20959
msgid ""
"In GNU Emacs, in Texinfo mode, the @code{texinfo-show-structure} command "
"lists all the lines that begin with the @@-commands that specify the "
"structure: @code{@@chapter}, @code{@@section}, @code{@@appendix}, and so "
"on.  With an argument (@w{@kbd{C-u}} as prefix argument, if interactive), "
"the command also shows the @code{@@node} lines.  The "
"@code{texinfo-show-structure} command is bound to @kbd{C-c C-s} in Texinfo "
"mode, by default."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20963
msgid ""
"The lines are displayed in a buffer called the @samp{*Occur*} buffer, "
"indented by hierarchical level.  For example, here is a part of what was "
"produced by running @code{texinfo-show-structure} on this manual:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20978
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Lines matching \"^@@\\\\(chapter \\\\|sect\\\\|subs\\\\|subh\\\\|\n"
"unnum\\\\|major\\\\|chapheading \\\\|heading \\\\|appendix\\\\)\"\n"
"in buffer texinfo.texi.\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"4177:@@chapter Nodes\n"
"4198:    @@heading Two Paths\n"
"4231:    @@section Node and Menu Illustration\n"
"4337:    @@section The @@code@{@@@@node@} Command\n"
"4393:        @@subheading Choosing Node and Pointer Names\n"
"4417:        @@subsection How to Write a @@code@{@@@@node@} Line\n"
"4469:        @@subsection @@code@{@@@@node@} Line Tips\n"
"@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20989
msgid ""
"This says that lines 4337, 4393, and 4417 of @file{texinfo.texi} begin with "
"the @code{@@section}, @code{@@subheading}, and @code{@@subsection} commands "
"respectively.  If you move your cursor into the @samp{*Occur*} window, you "
"can position the cursor over one of the lines and use the @kbd{C-c C-c} "
"command (@code{occur-mode-goto-occurrence}), to jump to the corresponding "
"spot in the Texinfo file.  @xref{Other Repeating Search, , Using Occur, "
"emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}, for more information about "
"@code{occur-mode-goto-occurrence}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:20995
msgid ""
"The first line in the @samp{*Occur*} window describes the @dfn{regular "
"expression} specified by @var{texinfo-heading-pattern}.  This regular "
"expression is the pattern that @code{texinfo-show-structure} looks for.  "
"@xref{Regexps, , Using Regular Expressions, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}, "
"for more information."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21003
msgid ""
"When you invoke the @code{texinfo-show-structure} command, Emacs will "
"display the structure of the whole buffer.  If you want to see the structure "
"of just a part of the buffer, of one chapter, for example, use the @kbd{C-x "
"n n} (@code{narrow-to-region}) command to mark the region.  "
"(@xref{Narrowing, , , emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}.)  This is how the "
"example used above was generated.  (To see the whole buffer again, use "
"@kbd{C-x n w} (@code{widen}).)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21008
msgid ""
"If you call @code{texinfo-show-structure} with a prefix argument by typing "
"@w{@kbd{C-u C-c C-s}}, it will list lines beginning with @code{@@node} as "
"well as the lines beginning with the @@-sign commands for @code{@@chapter}, "
"@code{@@section}, and the like."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21012
msgid ""
"You can remind yourself of the structure of a Texinfo file by looking at the "
"list in the @samp{*Occur*} window; and if you have mis-named a node or left "
"out a section, you can correct the mistake."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21014 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21016
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using @code{occur}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21016
msgid "@nodedescription How to list all lines containing a pattern."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21018
#, no-wrap
msgid "Occurrences, listing with @code{@@occur}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21019
#, no-wrap
msgid "occur"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21026
msgid ""
"Sometimes the @code{texinfo-show-structure} command produces too much "
"information.  Perhaps you want to remind yourself of the overall structure "
"of a Texinfo file, and are overwhelmed by the detailed list produced by "
"@code{texinfo-show-structure}.  In this case, you can use the @code{occur} "
"command directly.  To do this, type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21029
#, no-wrap
msgid "@kbd{M-x occur}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21038
msgid ""
"and then, when prompted, type a @dfn{regexp}, a regular expression for the "
"pattern you want to match.  (@xref{Regexps, , Regular Expressions, emacs, "
"The GNU Emacs Manual}.)  The @code{occur} command works from the current "
"location of the cursor in the buffer to the end of the buffer.  If you want "
"to run @code{occur} on the whole buffer, place the cursor at the beginning "
"of the buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21043
msgid ""
"For example, to see all the lines that contain the word @samp{@@chapter} in "
"them, just type @samp{@@chapter}.  This will produce a list of the "
"chapters.  It will also list all the sentences with @samp{@@chapter} in the "
"middle of the line."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21051
msgid ""
"If you want to see only those lines that start with the word "
"@samp{@@chapter}, type @samp{^@@chapter} when prompted by @code{occur}.  If "
"you want to see all the lines that end with a word or phrase, end the last "
"word with a @samp{$}; for example, @samp{catching mistakes$}.  This can be "
"helpful when you want to see all the nodes that are part of the same chapter "
"or section and therefore have the same `Up' pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21054
msgid ""
"@xref{Other Repeating Search, , Using Occur, emacs , The GNU Emacs Manual}, "
"for more information."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21056 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21058
#, no-wrap
msgid "Updating Nodes and Menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21058
msgid "@nodedescription How to update or create new nodes and menus."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21060
#, no-wrap
msgid "Updating nodes and menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21061
#, no-wrap
msgid "Create nodes, menus automatically"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21062
#, no-wrap
msgid "Insert nodes, menus automatically"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21063
#, no-wrap
msgid "Automatically insert nodes, menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21071
msgid ""
"The @command{texi2any} command will create an Info file for a hierarchically "
"organized Texinfo file that lacks `Next', `Previous' and `Up' pointers "
"(@pxref{Writing a Node}).  Thus, in general, there is no need for explicit "
"`Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers.  In this setting, menus will be added "
"automatically for nodes without an explicit menu.  (@xref{Generic Translator "
"@command{texi2any}}, for more information about @command{texi2any}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21078
msgid ""
"If you still want explicit pointers, Texinfo mode provides commands for "
"automatically creating or updating menus and node pointers.  The commands "
"are called ``update'' commands because their most frequent use is for "
"updating a Texinfo file after you have worked on it; but you can use them to "
"insert the `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers into an @code{@@node} line "
"that has none and to create menus in a file that has none."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21080
#, no-wrap
msgid "Updating Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21082
msgid "@nodedescription Five major updating commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21082
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Updating Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21085
msgid "You can use the updating commands to:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21089
msgid "insert or update the `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers of a node,"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21092
msgid "insert or update the menu for a section, and"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21095
msgid "create a master menu for a Texinfo source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21099
msgid ""
"You can also use the commands to update all the nodes and menus in a region "
"or in a whole Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21106
msgid ""
"The updating commands work only with conventional Texinfo files, which are "
"structured hierarchically like books.  In such files, a structuring command "
"line must follow closely after each @code{@@node} line, except for the `Top' "
"@code{@@node} line.  (A @dfn{structuring command line} is a line beginning "
"with @code{@@chapter}, @code{@@section}, or other similar command.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21113
msgid ""
"You can write the structuring command line on the line that follows "
"immediately after an @code{@@node} line or else on the line that follows "
"after a single @code{@@comment} line or a single @code{@@ifinfo} line.  You "
"cannot interpose more than one line between the @code{@@node} line and the "
"structuring command line; and you may interpose only a @code{@@comment} line "
"or an @code{@@ifinfo} line."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21121
msgid ""
"Commands which work on a whole buffer require that the `Top' node be "
"followed by a node with a @code{@@chapter} or equivalent-level command.  The "
"menu updating commands will not create a main or master menu for a Texinfo "
"file that has only @code{@@chapter}-level nodes! The menu updating commands "
"only create menus @emph{within} nodes for lower level nodes.  To create a "
"menu of chapters, you must provide a `Top' node."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21127
msgid ""
"The menu updating commands remove menu entries that refer to other Info "
"files since they do not refer to nodes within the current buffer.  This is a "
"deficiency.  Rather than use menu entries, you can use cross references to "
"refer to other Info files.  None of the updating commands affect "
"cross-references."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21134
msgid ""
"Texinfo mode has five updating commands that are used most often: two are "
"for updating the node pointers or menu of a single node (or a region); two "
"are for updating every node pointer and menu in a file; and one, the "
"@code{texinfo-master-menu} command, is for creating a master menu for a "
"complete file, and optionally, for updating every node and menu in the whole "
"Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21136
msgid "The @code{texinfo-master-menu} command is the primary command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21138
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-master-menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21139
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-u m"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21140
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-master-menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21144
msgid ""
"Create or update a master menu that includes all the other menus "
"(incorporating the descriptions from pre-existing menus, if any)."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21149
msgid ""
"With an argument (prefix argument, @kbd{C-u,} if interactive), first create "
"or update all the nodes and all the regular menus in the buffer before "
"constructing the master menu.  (@xref{The Top Node, , The Top Node and "
"Master Menu}, for more about a master menu.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21152
msgid ""
"For @code{texinfo-master-menu} to work, the Texinfo file must have a `Top' "
"node and at least one subsequent node."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21154
msgid "After extensively editing a Texinfo file, you can type the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21157
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u M-x texinfo-master-menu\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21159
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u C-c C-u m\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21163
msgid "This updates all the nodes and menus completely and all at once."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21168
msgid ""
"The other major updating commands do smaller jobs and are designed for the "
"person who updates nodes and menus as he or she writes a Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21171
msgid "The commands are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21173
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-update-node"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21174
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-u C-n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21175
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-update-node"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21183
msgid ""
"Insert the `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers for the node that point is "
"within (i.e., for the @code{@@node} line preceding point).  If the "
"@code{@@node} line has pre-existing `Next', `Previous', or `Up' pointers in "
"it, the old pointers are removed and new ones inserted.  With an argument "
"(prefix argument, @kbd{C-u}, if interactive), this command updates all "
"@code{@@node} lines in the region (which is the text between point and mark)."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21184
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-make-menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21185
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-u C-m"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21186
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-make-menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21192
msgid ""
"Create or update the menu in the node that point is within.  With an "
"argument (@kbd{C-u} as prefix argument, if interactive), the command makes "
"or updates menus for the nodes which are either within or a part of the "
"region."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21198
msgid ""
"Whenever @code{texinfo-make-menu} updates an existing menu, the descriptions "
"from that menu are incorporated into the new menu.  This is done by copying "
"descriptions from the existing menu to the entries in the new menu that have "
"the same node names.  If the node names are different, the descriptions are "
"not copied to the new menu."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21199
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-every-node-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21200
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-u C-e"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21201
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-every-node-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21204
msgid ""
"Insert or update the `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers for every node in "
"the buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21205
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-all-menus-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21206
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-u C-a"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21207
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-all-menus-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21212
msgid ""
"Create or update all the menus in the buffer.  With an argument (@kbd{C-u} "
"as prefix argument, if interactive), first insert or update all the node "
"pointers before working on the menus."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21217
msgid ""
"If a master menu exists, the @code{texinfo-all-menus-update} command updates "
"it; but the command does not create a new master menu if none already "
"exists.  (Use the @code{texinfo-master-menu} command for that.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21220
msgid ""
"When working on a document that does not merit a master menu, you can type "
"the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21223
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u C-c C-u C-a\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21225
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u M-x texinfo-all-menus-update\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21229
msgid "This updates all the nodes and menus."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21238
msgid ""
"The @code{texinfo-column-for-description} variable specifies the column to "
"which menu descriptions are indented.  By default, the value is 32 although "
"it can be useful to reduce it to as low as 24.  You can set the variable via "
"customization (@pxref{Customization,,, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}) or with "
"the @kbd{M-x set-variable} command (@pxref{Examining, , Examining and "
"Setting Variables, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21250
msgid ""
"Also, the @code{texinfo-indent-menu-description} command may be used to "
"indent existing menu descriptions to a specified column.  Finally, if you "
"wish, you can use the @code{texinfo-insert-node-lines} command to insert "
"missing @code{@@node} lines into a file.  In particular, you can ignore "
"@code{@@node} lines altogether in your first draft and then use the "
"@code{texinfo-insert-node-lines} command to create @code{@@node} lines for "
"you.  However, we do not recommend this practice.  It is better to name the "
"node itself at the same time that you write a segment so you can easily make "
"cross-references.  Useful cross-references are an especially important "
"feature of a good Texinfo manual.  (@xref{Other Updating Commands}, for more "
"information.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21252 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21256
#, no-wrap
msgid "Updating Requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21255
msgid ""
"@nodedescriptionblock How to structure a Texinfo file for using the updating "
"command."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21257
#, no-wrap
msgid "Updating requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21258
#, no-wrap
msgid "Requirements for updating commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21268
msgid ""
"To use the updating commands, you must organize the Texinfo file "
"hierarchically with chapters, sections, subsections, and the like.  When you "
"construct the hierarchy of the manual, do not `jump down' more than one "
"level at a time: you can follow the `Top' node with a chapter, but not with "
"a section; you can follow a chapter with a section, but not with a "
"subsection.  However, you may `jump up' any number of levels at one "
"time---for example, from a subsection to a chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21273
msgid ""
"Each @code{@@node} line, with the exception of the line for the `Top' node, "
"must be followed by a line with a structuring command such as "
"@code{@@chapter}, @code{@@section}, or @code{@@unnumberedsubsec}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21276
msgid ""
"Each @code{@@node} line/structuring-command line combination must look "
"either like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21282
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node     Comments,  Minimum, Conventions, Overview\n"
"@@comment  node-name, next,    previous,    up\n"
"@@section Comments\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21286
msgid "or like this (without the @code{@@comment} line):"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21291
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Comments, Minimum, Conventions, Overview\n"
"@@section Comments\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21295
msgid "or like this (without the explicit node pointers):"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21300
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Comments\n"
"@@section Comments\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21308
msgid ""
"In this example, `Comments' is the name of both the node and the section.  "
"The next node is called `Minimum' and the previous node is called "
"`Conventions'.  The `Comments' section is within the `Overview' node, which "
"is specified by the `Up' pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21311
msgid ""
"If a file has a `Top' node, it must be called @samp{top} or @samp{Top} and "
"be the first node in the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21315
msgid ""
"The menu updating commands create a menu of sections within a chapter, a "
"menu of subsections within a section, and so on.  This means that you must "
"have a `Top' node if you want a menu of chapters."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21317
#, no-wrap
msgid "Update Multiple Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21320
msgid ""
"@nodedescriptionblock How to create and update nodes and menus when using "
"included files."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21321
#, no-wrap
msgid "Update Outer File and Include Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21324 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21325
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-multiple-files-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21334
msgid ""
"GNU Emacs Texinfo mode provides the @code{texinfo-multiple-files-update} "
"command.  This command creates or updates `Next', `Previous', and `Up' "
"pointers of included files as well as those in the outer or overall Texinfo "
"file, and it creates or updates a main menu in the outer file.  Depending on "
"whether you call it with optional arguments, the command updates only the "
"pointers in the first @code{@@node} line of the included files or all of "
"them."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21340
msgid ""
"With @kbd{C-u} as a prefix argument, create and insert a master menu in the "
"outer file.  With a numeric prefix argument, such as @kbd{C-u 2}, first "
"update all the menus and all the `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers of "
"all the included files before creating and inserting a master menu in the "
"outer file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21342
msgid "In more details:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21344
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-multiple-files-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21346
msgid "Called without any arguments:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21352
msgid ""
"Create or update the `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers of the first "
"@code{@@node} line in each file included in an outer or overall Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21356 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21392
msgid ""
"Create or update the `Top' level node pointers of the outer or overall file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21359
msgid "Create or update a main menu in the outer file."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21361
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u M-x texinfo-multiple-files-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21363
msgid "Called with @kbd{C-u} as a prefix argument:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21368
msgid ""
"Create or update pointers in the first @code{@@node} line in each included "
"file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21371
msgid "Create or update the `Top' level node pointers of the outer file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21375
msgid ""
"Create and insert a master menu in the outer file.  The master menu is made "
"from all the menus in all the included files."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21377
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u 8 M-x texinfo-multiple-files-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21379
msgid "Called with a numeric prefix argument, such as @kbd{C-u 8}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21384
msgid ""
"Create or update @emph{all} the `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers of all "
"the included files."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21388
msgid "Create or update @emph{all} the menus of all the included files."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21397
msgid ""
"And then create a master menu in the outer file.  This is similar to "
"invoking @code{texinfo-master-menu} with an argument when you are working "
"with just one file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21406
msgid ""
"Note the use of the prefix argument in interactive use: with a regular "
"prefix argument, just @w{@kbd{C-u}}, the "
"@code{texinfo-multiple-files-update} command inserts a master menu; with a "
"numeric prefix argument, such as @kbd{C-u 8}, the command updates "
"@emph{every} pointer and menu in @emph{all} the files and then inserts a "
"master menu."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21408 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21412
#, no-wrap
msgid "Include Files Requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21411
msgid ""
"@nodedescriptionblock How to structure outer file and include files for the "
"updating command."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21413
#, no-wrap
msgid "Include files requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21414
#, no-wrap
msgid "Requirements for include files"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21422
msgid ""
"If you plan to use the @code{texinfo-multiple-files-update} command, the "
"outer Texinfo file that lists included files within it should contain "
"nothing but the beginning and end parts of a Texinfo file, and a number of "
"@code{@@include} commands listing the included files.  It should not even "
"include indices, which should be listed in an included file of their own."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21431
msgid ""
"Moreover, each of the included files must contain exactly one highest level "
"node (conventionally, @code{@@chapter} or equivalent), and this node must be "
"the first node in the included file.  Furthermore, each of these highest "
"level nodes in each included file must be at the same hierarchical level in "
"the file structure.  Usually, each is a @code{@@chapter}, an "
"@code{@@appendix}, or an @code{@@unnumbered} node.  Thus, normally, each "
"included file contains one, and only one, chapter or equivalent-level node."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21436
msgid ""
"The outer file should contain only @emph{one} node, the `Top' node.  It "
"should @emph{not} contain any nodes besides the single `Top' node.  The "
"@code{texinfo-multiple-files-update} command will not process them."
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21438 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21443
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22006
#, no-wrap
msgid "Other Updating Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21442
msgid ""
"@nodedescriptionblock How to indent descriptions, insert missing nodes "
"lines, and update nodes in sequence."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21447
msgid ""
"In addition to the major updating commands, Texinfo mode possesses several "
"less frequently used updating commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21449
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-insert-node-lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21450
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-insert-node-lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21454
msgid ""
"Insert @code{@@node} lines before the @code{@@chapter}, @code{@@section}, "
"and other sectioning commands wherever they are missing throughout a region "
"in a Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21462
msgid ""
"With an argument (@kbd{C-u} as prefix argument, if interactive), the command "
"@code{texinfo-insert-node-lines} not only inserts @code{@@node} lines but "
"also inserts the chapter or section titles as the names of the corresponding "
"nodes.  In addition, it inserts the titles as node names in pre-existing "
"@code{@@node} lines that lack names.  Since node names should be more "
"concise than section or chapter titles, you must manually edit node names so "
"inserted."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21465
msgid ""
"For example, the following marks a whole buffer as a region and inserts "
"@code{@@node} lines and titles throughout:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21468
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-x h C-u M-x texinfo-insert-node-lines\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21475
msgid ""
"This command inserts titles as node names in @code{@@node} lines; the "
"@code{texinfo-start-menu-description} command (@pxref{Inserting, Inserting "
"Frequently Used Commands}) inserts titles as descriptions in menu entries, a "
"different action.  However, in both cases, you need to edit the inserted "
"text."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21476
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-indent-menu-description"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21477
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-indent-menu-description"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21485
msgid ""
"Indent every description in the menu following point to the specified "
"column.  You can use this command to give yourself more space for "
"descriptions.  With an argument (@kbd{C-u} as prefix argument, if "
"interactive), the @code{texinfo-indent-menu-description} command indents "
"every description in every menu in the region.  However, this command does "
"not indent the second and subsequent lines of a multi-line description."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21486
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-sequential-node-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21487
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-sequential-node-update"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21498
msgid ""
"Insert the names of the nodes immediately following and preceding the "
"current node as the `Next' or `Previous' pointers regardless of those nodes' "
"hierarchical level.  This means that the `Next' node of a subsection may "
"well be the next chapter.  Sequentially ordered nodes are useful for novels "
"and other documents that you read through sequentially.  (However, in Info, "
"the @kbd{g *} command lets you look through the file sequentially, so "
"sequentially ordered nodes are not strictly necessary.)  With an argument "
"(prefix argument, if interactive), the @code{texinfo-sequential-node-update} "
"command sequentially updates all the nodes in the region."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
