msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21523
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shell, running @command{makeinfo} in"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21524
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shell, running @command{texi2any} in"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21529
msgid ""
"The @command{texi2any} program provides better error messages than either of "
"the Emacs formatting commands.  We recommend it.  The @command{texi2any} "
"program is independent of Emacs."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21534
msgid ""
"You can run @command{texi2any} (or @command{makeinfo}) in GNU Emacs Texinfo "
"mode by using either the @code{makeinfo-region} or the "
"@code{makeinfo-buffer} commands.  In Texinfo mode, the commands are bound to "
"@kbd{C-c C-m C-r} and @kbd{C-c C-m C-b} by default."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21536
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-m C-r"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21537
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x makeinfo-region"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21539 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21648
msgid "Format the current region for Info."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21539
#, no-wrap
msgid "makeinfo-region"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21541
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-m C-b"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21542
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x makeinfo-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21544 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21653
msgid "Format the current buffer for Info."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21544
#, no-wrap
msgid "makeinfo-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21551
msgid ""
"When you invoke @code{makeinfo-region} the output goes to a temporary "
"buffer.  When you invoke @code{makeinfo-buffer} output goes to the file set "
"with @code{@@setfilename} (@pxref{@code{@@setfilename}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21556
msgid ""
"The Emacs @code{makeinfo-region} and @code{makeinfo-buffer} commands run the "
"@command{texi2any} program in a temporary shell buffer.  If "
"@command{texi2any} finds any errors, Emacs displays the error messages in "
"the temporary buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21557
#, no-wrap
msgid "Errors, parsing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21558
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parsing errors"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21559
#, no-wrap
msgid "next-error"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21566
msgid ""
"You can parse the error messages by typing @kbd{C-x `} (@code{next-error}).  "
"This causes Emacs to go to and position the cursor on the line in the "
"Texinfo source that @command{texi2any} thinks caused the error.  "
"@xref{Compilation, , Running @code{make} or Compilers Generally, emacs, The "
"GNU Emacs Manual}, for more information about using the @code{next-error} "
"command."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21570
msgid ""
"In addition, you can kill the shell in which the @command{texi2any} command "
"is running or make the shell buffer display its most recent output."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21572
#, no-wrap
msgid "makeinfo-kill-job"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21573
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-m C-k"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21574
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x makeinfo-kill-job"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21577
msgid ""
"Kill the current running @command{texi2any} (or @command{makeinfo}) job "
"(from @code{makeinfo-region} or @code{makeinfo-buffer})."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21578
#, no-wrap
msgid "makeinfo-recenter-output-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21579
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-m C-l"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21580
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x makeinfo-recenter-output-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21583
msgid ""
"Redisplay the @command{texi2any} shell buffer to display its most recent "
"output."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21589
msgid ""
"(Note that the parallel commands for killing and recentering a @TeX{} job "
"are @kbd{C-c C-t C-k} and @kbd{C-c C-t C-l}.  @xref{Texinfo Mode Printing}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21594
msgid ""
"You can specify options for @command{texi2any} by setting the "
"@code{makeinfo-options} variable with either the @kbd{M-x customize} or the "
"@kbd{M-x set-variable} command, or by setting the variable in your "
"@file{.emacs} initialization file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21596
msgid "For example, you could write the following in your @file{.emacs} file:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21602
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(setq makeinfo-options\n"
"     \"--paragraph-indent=0 --no-split\n"
"      --fill-column=70 --verbose\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: iftex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21612
msgid ""
"For more information, see @ref{@command{texi2any} Options}, as well as "
"``Easy Customization Interface,'' ``Examining and Setting Variables,'' and "
"``Init File'' in @cite{The GNU Emacs Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: ifnottex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21619
msgid ""
"For more information, see@* @ref{Easy Customization, , Easy Customization "
"Interface, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual},@* @ref{Examining, , Examining and "
"Setting Variables, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual},@* @ref{Init File, , , "
"emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}, and@* @ref{@command{texi2any} Options}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21622
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{texinfo-format} commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21626
msgid ""
"@nodedescriptionblock Two Info formatting commands written in Emacs Lisp are "
"an alternative to @command{texi2any}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21627
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @code{texinfo-format@dots{}} Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21635
msgid ""
"In GNU Emacs in Texinfo mode, you can format part or all of a Texinfo file "
"with the @code{texinfo-format-region} command.  This formats the current "
"region and displays the formatted text in a temporary buffer called "
"@samp{*Info Region*}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21642
msgid ""
"Similarly, you can format a buffer with the @code{texinfo-format-buffer} "
"command.  This command creates a new buffer and generates the Info file in "
"it.  Typing @kbd{C-x C-s} will save the Info file under the name specified "
"by the @code{@@setfilename} line which must be near the beginning of the "
"Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21644 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21646
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-format-region"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21645
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-e C-r"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21649 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21651
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-format-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21650
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-e C-b"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21661
msgid ""
"The @code{texinfo-format-region} and @code{texinfo-format-buffer} commands "
"provide you with some error checking, and other functions can provide you "
"with further help in finding formatting errors.  These procedures are "
"described in an appendix; see @ref{Catching Mistakes}.  However, the "
"@command{texi2any} program provides better error checking "
"(@pxref{@command{texi2any} in Emacs})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21665
msgid ""
"A peculiarity of the @code{texinfo-format-buffer} and "
"@code{texinfo-format-region} commands is that they do not indent (nor fill) "
"paragraphs that contain @code{@@w} or @code{@@*} commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21667
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printing with Emacs"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21669
msgid "@nodedescription How to format with @TeX{} and print with Emacs."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21669
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting and Printing with Emacs"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21674
msgid ""
"GNU Emacs can be used for formatting and printing with @TeX{}, from an Emacs "
"Shell.  Texinfo mode also provides predefined key commands for formatting "
"and printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21676
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo Mode Printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21678
msgid "@nodedescription How to format and print part or all of a file."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21678
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting and Printing in Texinfo Mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21679
#, no-wrap
msgid "Region printing in Texinfo mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21680
#, no-wrap
msgid "Format and print in Texinfo mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21681
#, no-wrap
msgid "Print and format in Texinfo mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21688
msgid ""
"Texinfo mode provides several predefined key commands for @TeX{} formatting "
"and printing.  These include commands for sorting indices, looking at the "
"printer queue, killing the formatting job, and recentering the display of "
"the buffer in which the operations occur."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21689
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printing a region or buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21690
#, no-wrap
msgid "Region formatting and printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21691
#, no-wrap
msgid "Buffer formatting and printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21692
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part of file formatting and printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21698
msgid ""
"Often, when you are writing a document, you want to typeset and print only "
"part of a file to see what it will look like.  You can use the "
"@code{texinfo-tex-region} and related commands for this purpose.  Use the "
"@code{texinfo-tex-buffer} command to format all of a buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21705
msgid ""
"For @code{texinfo-tex-region} or @code{texinfo-tex-buffer} to work, the file "
"@emph{must} start with a @samp{\\input texinfo} line and must include a "
"@code{@@settitle} line.  The file must end with @code{@@bye} on a line by "
"itself.  (When you use @code{texinfo-tex-region}, you must surround the "
"@code{@@settitle} line with start-of-header and end-of-header lines.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21707
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-tex-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21708
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-b"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21709
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-tex-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21713
msgid ""
"Run @command{texi2dvi} on the buffer.  In addition to running @TeX{} on the "
"buffer, this command automatically creates or updates indices as needed."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21714
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-r"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21715
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-tex-region"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21717
msgid "Run @TeX{} on the current region."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21724
msgid ""
"If @@-commands related to printed output are between the start-of-header and "
"end-of-header lines, then @code{texinfo-tex-region} will format the region "
"accordingly.  For example, if you write the @code{@@smallbook} command "
"between the start-of-header and end-of-header lines, "
"@code{texinfo-tex-region}, will format the region in ``small'' book size."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21725
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-i"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21726
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-texindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21735
msgid ""
"Run @command{texindex} to sort the indices of a Texinfo file formatted with "
"@code{texinfo-tex-region}.  The @code{texinfo-tex-region} command does not "
"run @command{texindex} automatically; it only runs the @command{tex} "
"typesetting command.  You must run the @code{texinfo-tex-region} command a "
"second time after sorting the raw index files with the @command{texindex} "
"command.  (Usually, you do not format an index when you format a region, "
"only when you format a buffer.  Now that the @command{texi2dvi} command "
"exists, there is little or no need for this command.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21736
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-p"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21737
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-tex-print"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21740
msgid ""
"Print a DVI file that was made with @code{texinfo-tex-region} or "
"@code{texinfo-tex-buffer}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21741
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-q"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21742
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x tex-show-print-queue"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21744
msgid "Show the print queue."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21745
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-d"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21746
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-delete-from-print-queue"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21750
msgid ""
"Delete a job from the print queue; you will be prompted for the job number "
"shown by a preceding @kbd{C-c C-t C-q} command "
"(@code{texinfo-show-tex-print-queue})."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21751
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-k"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21752
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x tex-kill-job"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21756
msgid ""
"Kill the currently running @TeX{} job started by either "
"@code{texinfo-tex-region} or @code{texinfo-tex-buffer}, or any other process "
"running in the Texinfo shell buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21757
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-x"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21758
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-quit-job"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21762
msgid ""
"Quit a @TeX{} formatting job that has stopped because of an error by sending "
"an @key{x} to it.  When you do this, @TeX{} preserves a record of what it "
"did in a @file{.log} file."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21763
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-l"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21764
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x tex-recenter-output-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21767
msgid ""
"Redisplay the shell buffer in which the @TeX{} printing and formatting "
"commands are run to show its most recent output."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21772
msgid ""
"Thus, the usual sequence of commands for formatting a buffer is as follows "
"(with comments to the right):"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21778
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-t C-b             @r{Run @command{texi2dvi} on the buffer.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-p             @r{Print the DVI file.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-q             @r{Display the printer queue.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21785
msgid ""
"The Texinfo mode @TeX{} formatting commands start a subshell in Emacs called "
"the @file{*tex-shell*}.  The @code{texinfo-tex-command}, "
"@code{texinfo-texindex-command}, and @code{tex-dvi-print-command} commands "
"are all run in this shell."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21789
msgid ""
"You can watch the commands operate in the @samp{*tex-shell*} buffer, and you "
"can switch to and from and use the @samp{*tex-shell*} buffer as you would "
"any other shell buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21793
msgid ""
"The formatting and print commands depend on the values of several "
"variables.  The default values are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21797
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    @r{Variable}                              @r{Default value}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21807
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"texinfo-texi2dvi-command                  \"texi2dvi\"\n"
"texinfo-tex-command                       \"tex\"\n"
"texinfo-texindex-command                  \"texindex\"\n"
"texinfo-delete-from-print-queue-command   \"lprm\"\n"
"texinfo-tex-trailer                       \"@@bye\"\n"
"tex-start-of-header                       \"%**start\"\n"
"tex-end-of-header                         \"%**end\"\n"
"tex-dvi-print-command                     \"lpr -d\"\n"
"tex-show-queue-command                    \"lpq\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21815
msgid ""
"You can change the values of these variables with the @kbd{M-x set-variable} "
"command (@pxref{Examining, , Examining and Setting Variables, emacs, The GNU "
"Emacs Manual}), or with your @file{.emacs} initialization file (@pxref{Init "
"File, , , emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual})."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21816
#, no-wrap
msgid "Customize Emacs package (@t{Development/Docs/Texinfo})"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21823
msgid ""
"Beginning with version 20, GNU Emacs offers a user-friendly interface, "
"called @dfn{Customize}, for changing values of user-definable variables.  "
"@xref{Easy Customization, , Easy Customization Interface, emacs, The GNU "
"Emacs Manual}, for more details about this.  The Texinfo variables can be "
"found in the @samp{Development/Docs/Texinfo} group, once you invoke the "
"@kbd{M-x customize} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21825
#, no-wrap
msgid "Compile-Command"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21827
msgid "@nodedescription How to print using Emacs's compile command."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21827
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using the Local Variables List"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21828
#, no-wrap
msgid "Local variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21829
#, no-wrap
msgid "Compile command for formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21830
#, no-wrap
msgid "Format with the compile command"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21840
msgid ""
"Yet another way to apply the @TeX{} formatting command to a Texinfo file is "
"to put that command in a @dfn{local variables list} at the end of the "
"Texinfo file.  You can then specify the @command{tex} or @command{texi2dvi} "
"commands as a @code{compile-command} and have Emacs run it by typing "
"@kbd{M-x compile}.  This creates a special shell called the "
"@file{*compilation*} buffer in which Emacs runs the compile command.  For "
"example, at the end of the @file{gdb.texi} file, after the @code{@@bye}, you "
"could put the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21846
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Local Variables:\n"
"compile-command: \"texi2dvi gdb.texi\"\n"
"End:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21852
msgid ""
"This technique is most often used by programmers who also compile programs "
"this way; see @ref{Compilation, , , emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21856 texinfo/texinfo.texi:21858
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo Mode Summary"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21858
msgid "@nodedescription Summary of all the Texinfo mode commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21864
msgid ""
"In Texinfo mode, each set of commands has default keybindings that begin "
"with the same keys.  All the commands that are custom-created for Texinfo "
"mode begin with @kbd{C-c}.  The keys are somewhat mnemonic."
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21865
#, no-wrap
msgid "Insert Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21871
msgid ""
"The insert commands are invoked by typing @kbd{C-c} twice and then the first "
"letter of the @@-command to be inserted.  (It might make more sense "
"mnemonically to use @kbd{C-c C-i}, for `custom insert', but @kbd{C-c C-c} is "
"quick to type.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21883
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-c c       @r{Insert} @samp{@@code}.\n"
"C-c C-c d       @r{Insert} @samp{@@dfn}.\n"
"C-c C-c e       @r{Insert} @samp{@@end}.\n"
"C-c C-c i       @r{Insert} @samp{@@item}.\n"
"C-c C-c n       @r{Insert} @samp{@@node}.\n"
"C-c C-c s       @r{Insert} @samp{@@samp}.\n"
"C-c C-c v       @r{Insert} @samp{@@var}.\n"
"C-c @{       @r{Insert braces.}\n"
"C-c ]\n"
"C-c @}       @r{Move out of enclosing braces.}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21888
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-c C-d     @r{Insert a node's section title}\n"
"               @r{in the space for the description}\n"
"               @r{in a menu entry line.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21891
#, no-wrap
msgid "Show Structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21895
msgid ""
"The @code{texinfo-show-structure} command is often used within a narrowed "
"region."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21898
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-s         @r{List all the headings.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21900
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Master Update Command"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21904
msgid ""
"The @code{texinfo-master-menu} command creates a master menu; and can be "
"used to update every node and menu in a file as well."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21911
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-u m\n"
"M-x texinfo-master-menu\n"
"               @r{Create or update a master menu.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21917
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-u C-c C-u m   @r{With @kbd{C-u} as a prefix argument, first}\n"
"               @r{create or update all nodes and regular}\n"
"               @r{menus, and then create a master menu.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21920
#, no-wrap
msgid "Update Pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21925
msgid ""
"The update pointer commands are invoked by typing @kbd{C-c C-u} and then "
"either @kbd{C-n} for @code{texinfo-update-node} or @kbd{C-e} for "
"@code{texinfo-every-node-update}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21929
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-u C-n     @r{Update a node.}\n"
"C-c C-u C-e     @r{Update every node in the buffer.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21931
#, no-wrap
msgid "Update Menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21938
msgid ""
"Invoke the update menu commands by typing @kbd{C-c C-u} and then either "
"@kbd{C-m} for @code{texinfo-make-menu} or @kbd{C-a} for "
"@code{texinfo-all-menus-update}.  To update both nodes and menus at the same "
"time, precede @kbd{C-c C-u C-a} with @kbd{C-u}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21941
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-u C-m     @r{Make or update a menu.}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21945
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-u C-a     @r{Make or update all}\n"
"               @r{menus in a buffer.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21951
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-u C-c C-u C-a @r{With @kbd{C-u} as a prefix argument,}\n"
"               @r{first create or update all nodes and}\n"
"               @r{then create or update all menus.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21954
#, no-wrap
msgid "Format for Info"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21959
msgid ""
"The Info formatting commands that are written in Emacs Lisp are invoked by "
"typing @kbd{C-c C-e} and then either @kbd{C-r} for a region or @kbd{C-b} for "
"the whole buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21964
msgid ""
"The Info formatting commands that are based on the "
"@command{texi2any}/@command{makeinfo} program are invoked by typing @kbd{C-c "
"C-m} and then either @kbd{C-r} for a region or @kbd{C-b} for the whole "
"buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21968
msgid "Use the @code{texinfo-format@dots{}} commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21973
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-e C-r     @r{Format the region.}\n"
"C-c C-e C-b     @r{Format the buffer.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21979
msgid "Use @command{texi2any}/@command{makeinfo}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21985
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-m C-r     @r{Format the region.}\n"
"C-c C-m C-b     @r{Format the buffer.}\n"
"C-c C-m C-l     @r{Recenter the @command{texi2any} output buffer.}\n"
"C-c C-m C-k     @r{Kill the @command{texi2any} formatting job.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21987
#, no-wrap
msgid "Typeset and Print"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:21993
msgid ""
"The @TeX{} typesetting and printing commands are invoked by typing @kbd{C-c "
"C-t} and then another control command: @kbd{C-r} for "
"@code{texinfo-tex-region}, @kbd{C-b} for @code{texinfo-tex-buffer}, and so "
"on."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22004
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-t C-r     @r{Run @TeX{} on the region.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-b     @r{Run} @command{texi2dvi} @r{on the buffer.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-i     @r{Run} @command{texindex}.\n"
"C-c C-t C-p     @r{Print the DVI file.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-q     @r{Show the print queue.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-d     @r{Delete a job from the print queue.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-k     @r{Kill the current @TeX{} formatting job.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-x     @r{Quit a currently stopped @TeX{} formatting job.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-l     @r{Recenter the output buffer.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22010
msgid ""
"The remaining updating commands do not have standard keybindings because "
"they are rarely used."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22017
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x texinfo-insert-node-lines\n"
"               @r{Insert missing @code{@@node} lines in region.}\n"
"               @r{With @kbd{C-u} as a prefix argument,}\n"
"               @r{use section titles as node names.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22025
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x texinfo-multiple-files-update\n"
"               @r{Update a multi-file document.}\n"
"               @r{With @kbd{C-u 2} as a prefix argument,}\n"
"               @r{create or update all nodes and menus}\n"
"               @r{in all included files first.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22030
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x texinfo-indent-menu-description\n"
"               @r{Indent descriptions.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22035
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x texinfo-sequential-node-update\n"
"               @r{Insert node pointers in strict sequence.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22039
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting Info files"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22041
msgid "@nodedescription Processing directly Info files."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22041
#, no-wrap
msgid "Direct Formatting of Info files"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22043
#, no-wrap
msgid "Direct formatting of Info files"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22051
msgid ""
"In general, there is no need to edit or process Info files.  Most of the "
"content of Info file is plain text, but the tag tables require computing "
"offsets for nodes and indirect files (@pxref{Tag and Split Files}), which is "
"impractical.  The Texinfo processors output well-formatted Info from Texinfo "
"input.  Also, lot of information from the Texinfo source is lost in the Info "
"file.  The Texinfo source is therefore more suitable to analyse a manual, be "
"it for error reporting, for statistics, or to set up translations."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22059
msgid ""
"Still, especially with manually written Info files, there are some "
"situations where it may be interesting to process Info files directly, in "
"particular to compute Info files node offsets and to compute split Info "
"files file offsets.  Two Emacs commands do that.  @code{Info-tagify} adds a "
"tag table for a nonsplit file lacking one.  This function is also useful for "
"Info file validation (@pxref{Running @code{Info-validate}}).  It is also "
"possible to split a nonsplit Info file with @code{Info-split}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22061
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tagifying"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22063
msgid "@nodedescription How to tagify a file."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22063
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tagifying a File"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22064
#, no-wrap
msgid "Making a tag table manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22065
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tag table, making manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22066
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info files @subentry making a tag table"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22074
msgid ""
"Texinfo processors create tag tables automatically.  The only exception "
"arise when @code{texinfo-format-buffer} is called with a prefix, as @kbd{C-u "
"M-x texinfo-format-buffer}, to create an non split Info file without a tag "
"table (which can be useful in some cases for Info file validation, "
"@pxref{Unsplit and Tagify}).  Adding a tag table is therefore more generally "
"useful for manually written Info files."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22077
msgid ""
"To create a tag table for an nonsplit file, visit the Info file you wish to "
"tagify and type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22080 texinfo/texinfo.texi:22552
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x Info-tagify\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22087
msgid ""
"(Note the uppercase @samp{I} in @code{Info-tagify}.)  This creates an Info "
"file with a tag table.  A tag table is part of a well formed Info file "
"(@pxref{Tag and Split Files}).  A tag table is also needed to validate or "
"split the Info file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22089
#, no-wrap
msgid "Splitting"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22091
msgid "@nodedescription How to split a file manually."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22091
#, no-wrap
msgid "Splitting a File Manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22092
#, no-wrap
msgid "Splitting an Info file manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22093
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info files @subentry splitting manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22099
msgid ""
"You should split a large file or else let the @code{texinfo-format-buffer} "
"or @code{makeinfo-buffer} command do it for you automatically.  (Generally "
"you will let one of the formatting commands do this job for you.  @xref{Info "
"Formatting}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22101
msgid "The split-off files are called the indirect subfiles."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22106
msgid ""
"Tag tables are created automatically by the formatting command; you only "
"need to create a tag table yourself if you are doing the job manually.  "
"@xref{Tagifying}, for information about creating a tag table."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22109
msgid ""
"Visit the Info file you wish to tagify and split manually and type the two "
"commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22113
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x Info-tagify\n"
"M-x Info-split\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22117
msgid "(Note that the @samp{I} in @samp{Info} is uppercase.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22123
msgid ""
"When you use the @code{Info-split} command, the buffer is modified into a "
"(small) Info file which lists the indirect subfiles.  This file should be "
"saved in place of the original visited file.  The indirect subfiles are "
"written in the same directory the original file is in, with names generated "
"by appending @samp{-} and a number to the original file name."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22126
msgid ""
"The primary file still functions as an Info file, but it contains just the "
"tag table and a directory of subfiles.  @xref{Tag and Split Files}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22128 texinfo/texinfo.texi:22130
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching Mistakes"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22130
msgid "@nodedescription How to find mistakes in formatting."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22131
#, no-wrap
msgid "Structure, catching mistakes in"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22132
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nodes, catching mistakes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22133
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching mistakes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22134
#, no-wrap
msgid "Correcting mistakes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22135
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mistakes, catching"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22136
#, no-wrap
msgid "Problems, catching"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22137
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging the Texinfo structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22143
msgid ""
"Besides mistakes in the content of your documentation, there are two kinds "
"of mistake you can make with Texinfo: you can make mistakes with "
"@@-commands, and you can make mistakes with the structure of the nodes and "
"chapters."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22147
msgid ""
"For finding problems with @@-commands, you can run @TeX{} or a region "
"formatting command on the region that has a problem; indeed, you can run "
"these commands on each region as you write it."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22151
msgid ""
"For finding problems with the structure of nodes and chapters, you can use "
"@kbd{C-c C-s} (@code{texinfo-show-structure}) and the related @code{occur} "
"command and you can use the @kbd{M-x Info-validate} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22153 texinfo/texinfo.texi:22155
#, no-wrap
msgid "@command{texi2any} Preferred"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22155
msgid "@nodedescription @command{texi2any} finds errors."
msgstr ""

#.  prev name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22159
msgid "@code{makeinfo} Preferred"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22165
msgid ""
"The @command{texi2any} program does an excellent job of catching errors and "
"reporting them---far better than @code{texinfo-format-region} or "
"@code{texinfo-format-buffer}.  In addition, the various functions for "
"automatically creating and updating node pointers and menus remove many "
"opportunities for human error."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22172
msgid ""
"Use @command{texi2any} (or its Texinfo mode manifestations, "
"@code{makeinfo-region} and @code{makeinfo-buffer}) to format your file and "
"check for other errors.  This is the best way to work with Texinfo.  But if "
"you cannot use @command{texi2any}, or your problem is very puzzling, then "
"you may want to use the tools described in this section."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22174
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging with Info"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22176
msgid "@nodedescription How to catch errors with Info formatting."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22176
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching Errors with Info Formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22177
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching errors with Info formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22178
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging with Info formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22183
msgid ""
"After you have written part of a Texinfo file, you can use the "
"@code{texinfo-format-region} or the @code{makeinfo-region} command to see "
"whether the region formats properly."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22188
msgid ""
"Most likely, however, you are reading this section because for some reason "
"you cannot use the @code{makeinfo-region} command; therefore, the rest of "
"this section presumes that you are using @code{texinfo-format-region}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22196
msgid ""
"If you have made a mistake with an @@-command, @code{texinfo-format-region} "
"will stop processing at or after the error and display an error message.  To "
"see where in the buffer the error occurred, switch to the @samp{*Info "
"Region*} buffer; the cursor will be in a position that is after the location "
"of the error.  Also, the text will not be formatted after the place where "
"the error occurred (or more precisely, where it was detected)."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22200
msgid ""
"For example, if you accidentally end a menu with the command @code{@@end "
"menus} with an `s' on the end, instead of with @code{@@end menu}, you will "
"see an error message that says:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22203
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@end menus is not handled by texinfo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22208
msgid ""
"The cursor will stop at the point in the buffer where the error occurs, or "
"not long after it.  The buffer will look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22213
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"---------- Buffer: *Info Region* ----------\n"
"* Menu:\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22222
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Using texinfo-show-structure::  How to use\n"
"                                 `texinfo-show-structure'\n"
"                                 to catch mistakes.\n"
"* Running Info-validate::         How to check for\n"
"                                 unreferenced nodes.\n"
"@@end menus\n"
"@point{}\n"
"---------- Buffer: *Info Region* ----------\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22228
msgid ""
"The @code{texinfo-format-region} command sometimes provides slightly odd "
"error messages.  For example, the following cross-reference fails to format:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22231
#, no-wrap
msgid "(@@xref@{Catching Mistakes, for more info.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22239
msgid ""
"In this case, @code{texinfo-format-region} detects the missing closing brace "
"but displays a message that says @samp{Unbalanced parentheses} rather than "
"@samp{Unbalanced braces}.  This is because the formatting command looks for "
"mismatches between braces as if they were parentheses."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22243
msgid ""
"Sometimes @code{texinfo-format-region} fails to detect mistakes.  For "
"example, in the following, the closing brace is swapped with the closing "
"parenthesis:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22246
#, no-wrap
msgid "(@@xref@{Catching Mistakes), for more info.@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22250
msgid "Formatting produces:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22252
#, no-wrap
msgid "(*Note for more info.: Catching Mistakes)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22256
msgid ""
"The only way for you to detect this error is to realize that the reference "
"should have looked like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22259
#, no-wrap
msgid "(*Note Catching Mistakes::, for more info.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22264
msgid ""
"Incidentally, if you are reading this node in Info and type @kbd{f "
"@key{RET}} (@code{Info-follow-reference}), you will generate an error "
"message that says:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22267
#, no-wrap
msgid "No such node: \"Catching Mistakes) The only way @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22278
msgid ""
"This is because Info perceives the example of the error as the first "
"cross-reference in this node and if you type a @key{RET} immediately after "
"typing the Info @kbd{f} command, Info will attempt to go to the referenced "
"node.  If you type @kbd{f catch @key{TAB} @key{RET}}, Info will complete the "
"node name of the correctly written example and take you to the `Catching "
"Mistakes' node.  (If you try this, you can return from the `Catching "
"Mistakes' node by typing @kbd{l} (@code{Info-last}).)"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22280 texinfo/texinfo.texi:22282
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging with @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22282
msgid "@nodedescription How to catch errors with @TeX{} formatting."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22283
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching errors with @TeX{} formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22284
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging with @TeX{} formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22287
msgid "You can also catch mistakes when you format a file with @TeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22293
msgid ""
"Usually, you will want to do this after you have run "
"@code{texinfo-format-buffer} (or, better, @code{makeinfo-buffer}) on the "
"same file, because @code{texinfo-format-buffer} sometimes displays error "
"messages that make more sense than @TeX{}.  (@xref{Debugging with Info}, for "
"more information.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22296
msgid ""
"For example, @TeX{} was run on a Texinfo file, part of which is shown here:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22305
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"---------- Buffer: texinfo.texi ----------\n"
"name of the Texinfo file as an extension.  The\n"
"@@samp@{??@} are `wildcards' that cause the shell to\n"
"substitute all the raw index files.  (@@xref@{sorting\n"
"indices, for more information about sorting\n"
"indices.)\n"
"---------- Buffer: texinfo.texi ----------\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22310
msgid ""
"(The cross-reference lacks a closing brace.)  @TeX{} produced the following "
"output, after which it stopped:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22320
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"---------- Buffer: *tex-shell* ----------\n"
"Runaway argument?\n"
"@{sorting indices, for more information about sorting\n"
"indices.) @@ETC.\n"
"! Paragraph ended before @@xref was complete.\n"
"<to be read again>\n"
"                  @@par\n"
"l.27\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22323
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"?\n"
"---------- Buffer: *tex-shell* ----------\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22327
msgid ""
"In this case, @TeX{} produced an accurate and understandable error message:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22330
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paragraph ended before @@xref was complete.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22337
msgid ""
"@samp{@@par} is an internal @TeX{} command of no relevance to Texinfo.  "
"@samp{l.27} means that @TeX{} detected the problem on line 27 of the Texinfo "
"file.  The @samp{?} is the prompt @TeX{} uses in this circumstance."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22340
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, @TeX{} is not always so helpful, and sometimes you must truly "
"be a Sherlock Holmes to discover what went wrong."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22343
msgid ""
"In any case, if you run into a problem like this, you can do one of three "
"things."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22348
msgid ""
"You can tell @TeX{} to continue running and ignore just this error by typing "
"@key{RET} at the @samp{?} prompt."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22352
msgid ""
"You can tell @TeX{} to continue running and to ignore all errors as best it "
"can by typing @kbd{r @key{RET}} at the @samp{?} prompt."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22358
msgid ""
"This is often the best thing to do.  However, beware: the one error may "
"produce a cascade of additional error messages as its consequences are felt "
"through the rest of the file.  To stop @TeX{} when it is producing such an "
"avalanche of error messages, type @kbd{C-c} (or @kbd{C-c C-c}, if you are "
"running a shell inside Emacs)."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22362
msgid ""
"You can tell @TeX{} to stop this run by typing @kbd{x @key{RET}} at the "
"@samp{?} prompt."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22366
msgid ""
"If you are running @TeX{} inside Emacs, you need to switch to the shell "
"buffer and line at which @TeX{} offers the @samp{?} prompt."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22373
msgid ""
"Sometimes @TeX{} will format a file without producing error messages even "
"though there is a problem.  This usually occurs if a command is not ended "
"but @TeX{} is able to continue processing anyhow.  For example, if you fail "
"to end an itemized list with the @code{@@end itemize} command, @TeX{} will "
"write a DVI file that you can print out.  The only error message that @TeX{} "
"will give you is the somewhat mysterious comment:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22376
#, no-wrap
msgid "(@@end occurred inside a group at level 1)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22385
msgid ""
"However, if you print the DVI file, you will find that the text of the file "
"that follows the itemized list is entirely indented as if it were part of "
"the last item in the itemized list.  The error message is the way @TeX{} "
"says that it expected to find an @code{@@end} command somewhere in the file; "
"but that it could not determine where it was needed."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22390
msgid ""
"Another source of notoriously hard-to-find errors is a missing @code{@@end "
"group} command.  If you ever are stumped by incomprehensible errors, look "
"for a missing @code{@@end group} command first."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22395
msgid ""
"If the Texinfo file lacks header lines, @TeX{} may stop in the beginning of "
"its run and display output that looks like the following.  The @samp{*} "
"indicates that @TeX{} is waiting for input."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22400
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is TeX, Version 3.14159 (Web2c 7.0)\n"
"(test.texinfo [1])\n"
"*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22408
msgid ""
"In this case, simply type @kbd{\\end @key{RET}} after the asterisk.  Then "
"write the header lines in the Texinfo file and run the @TeX{} command again. "
"(Note the use of the backslash, @samp{\\}.  @TeX{} uses @samp{\\} instead of "
"@samp{@@}; and in this circumstance, you are working directly with @TeX{}, "
"not with Texinfo.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22410
#, no-wrap
msgid "Running @code{Info-validate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22412
msgid "@nodedescription How to find badly referenced nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22412
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finding Badly Referenced Nodes"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22415
msgid "Running Info-Validate"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22415
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info-validate"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22416
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nodes, checking for badly referenced"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22417
#, no-wrap
msgid "Checking for badly referenced nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22418
#, no-wrap
msgid "Looking for badly referenced nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22419
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finding badly referenced nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22420
#, no-wrap
msgid "Badly referenced nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22427
msgid ""
"You can use the @code{Info-validate} command to check whether any of the "
"`Next', `Previous', `Up' or other node pointers fail to point to a node.  "
"This command checks that every node pointer points to an existing node.  The "
"@code{Info-validate} command works only on Info files, not on Texinfo files."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22437
msgid ""
"The @command{texi2any} program validates pointers automatically, so you do "
"not need to use the @code{Info-validate} command if you are using "
"@command{texi2any}.  With the customization variable "
"@code{CHECK_NORMAL_MENU_STRUCTURE} set, @command{texi2any} will also warn if "
"the nodes pointers (either explicitly or automatically set)  are not "
"consistent with the order of node menu entries.  @command{texi2any} does not "
"check that every `Next' pointer is matched by a `Previous' (in the node "
"where the `Next' points) which points back, since it may be correct for a "
"non standard document structure."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22442
msgid ""
"You only may need to use @code{Info-validate} if you are unable to run "
"@command{texi2any} and instead must create an Info file using "
"@code{texinfo-format-region} or @code{texinfo-format-buffer}, or if you "
"write an Info file from scratch."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22444 texinfo/texinfo.texi:22446
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22448
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using @code{Info-validate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22446
msgid "@nodedescription How to run @code{Info-validate}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22449
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info validating a large file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22450
#, no-wrap
msgid "Validating a large file"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22454
msgid ""
"To use @code{Info-validate}, visit the Info file you wish to check and type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22457 texinfo/texinfo.texi:22558
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x Info-validate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22463
msgid ""
"Note that the @code{Info-validate} command requires an uppercase `I'@.  You "
"may also need to create a tag table before running @code{Info-validate}.  "
"@xref{Tagifying}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22468
msgid ""
"If your file is valid, you will receive a message that says ``File appears "
"valid''.  However, if you have a pointer that does not point to a node, "
"error messages will be displayed in a buffer called @samp{*problems in info "
"file*}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22471
msgid ""
"For example, @code{Info-validate} was run on a test file that contained only "
"the first node of this manual.  One of the messages said:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22474
#, no-wrap
msgid "In node \"Overview\", invalid Next: Texinfo Mode\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22480
msgid ""
"This meant that the node called @samp{Overview} had a `Next' pointer that "
"did not point to anything (which was true in this case, since the test file "
"had only one node in it)."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22484
msgid ""
"Now suppose we add a node named @samp{Texinfo Mode} to our test case but we "
"do not specify a `Previous' for this node.  Then we will get the following "
"error message:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22487
#, no-wrap
msgid "In node \"Texinfo Mode\", should have Previous: Overview\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22493
msgid ""
"This is because, with a standard document structure, every `Next' pointer "
"should be matched by a `Previous' (in the node where the `Next' points)  "
"which points back."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22496
msgid ""
"@code{Info-validate} also checks that all menu entries and cross-references "
"point to actual nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22503
msgid ""
"@code{Info-validate} requires a tag table and does not work with files that "
"have been split.  (The @code{texinfo-format-buffer} command automatically "
"splits large files.)  In order to use @code{Info-validate} on a large file, "
"you must run @code{texinfo-format-buffer} with an argument so that it does "
"not split the Info file; and you must create a tag table for the unsplit "
"file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22505
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unsplit and Tagify"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22508
msgid ""
"@nodedescriptionblock How to create an unsplit file and add a tag table for "
"validation."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22509
#, no-wrap
msgid "Creating an Unsplit File and Adding a Tag Table"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22511
msgid "Unsplit"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22512
#, no-wrap
msgid "Creating an unsplit file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22513
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unsplit file creation"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22525
msgid ""
"You can run @code{Info-validate} only on a single Info file that has a tag "
"table.  The command will not work on the indirect subfiles that are "
"generated when a master file is split.  If you have a large file (longer "
"than 300,000 bytes or so), you need to run the @code{texinfo-format-buffer} "
"or @code{makeinfo-buffer} command in such a way that it does not create "
"indirect subfiles.  You will also need to create a tag table for the Info "
"file.  After you have done this, you can run @code{Info-validate} and look "
"for badly referenced nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22530
msgid ""
"The first step is to create an unsplit Info file.  To prevent "
"@code{texinfo-format-buffer} from splitting a Texinfo file into smaller Info "
"files, give a prefix to the @kbd{M-x texinfo-format-buffer} command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22533
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u M-x texinfo-format-buffer\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22537
msgid "or else"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22540
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u C-c C-e C-b\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22545
msgid ""
"When you do this, @code{texinfo-format-buffer} will not split the file and "
"will not create a tag table for it."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22549
msgid ""
"After creating an unsplit Info file, you must create a tag table for it "
"(@pxref{Tagifying}).  Visit the unsplit Info file created by "
"@code{texinfo-format-buffer} and type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22555
msgid "The third step is to validate the Info file:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22563
msgid ""
"(Note the uppercase @samp{I} in @code{Info-validate}.)  In brief, the steps "
"are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22569
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-u M-x texinfo-format-buffer\n"
"M-x Info-tagify\n"
"M-x Info-validate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22576
msgid ""
"After you have validated the node structure, you can rerun "
"@code{texinfo-format-buffer} in the normal way so it will construct a tag "
"table and split the file automatically, or you can make the tag table and "
"split the file manually (@pxref{Splitting})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22578 texinfo/texinfo.texi:22580
#, no-wrap
msgid "Batch Formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22580
msgid "@nodedescription How to format for Info in Emacs batch mode."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22581
#, no-wrap
msgid "Batch formatting for Info"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22582
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info batch formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22588
msgid ""
"You can format Texinfo files for Info using @code{batch-texinfo-format} and "
"Emacs batch mode.  You can run Emacs in batch mode from any shell, including "
"a shell inside of Emacs.  (@xref{Initial Options,,, emacs, The GNU Emacs "
"Manual}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22591
msgid ""
"Here is a shell command to format all the files that end in @file{.texinfo} "
"in the current directory:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22594
#, no-wrap
msgid "emacs -batch -funcall batch-texinfo-format *.texinfo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22599
msgid ""
"Emacs processes all the files listed on the command line, even if an error "
"occurs while attempting to format some of them."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22602
msgid ""
"Run @code{batch-texinfo-format} only with Emacs in batch mode as shown; it "
"is not interactive.  It kills the batch mode Emacs on completion."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22609
msgid ""
"@code{batch-texinfo-format} is convenient if you lack @command{texi2any} and "
"want to format several Texinfo files at once.  When you use Batch mode, you "
"create a new Emacs process.  This frees your current Emacs, so you can "
"continue working in it.  (When you run @code{texinfo-format-region} or "
"@code{texinfo-format-buffer}, you cannot use that Emacs for anything else "
"until the command finishes.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22611 texinfo/texinfo.texi:22613
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22614
#, no-wrap
msgid "Global Document Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22613
msgid "@nodedescription Affecting formatting throughout."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22619
msgid ""
"Here are additional commands which affect the document as a whole.  Most of "
"these commands are for customizing the appearance of the printed output.  "
"They are generally all given before the Top node, if they are given at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22621
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@setchapternewpage}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22623
msgid "@nodedescription Start chapters on right-hand pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22623
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@setchapternewpage}: Blank Pages Before Chapters"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22626
#, no-wrap
msgid "setchapternewpage"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22627
#, no-wrap
msgid "Starting chapters"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22628
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pages, starting odd"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22636
msgid ""
"In an officially bound book, text is usually printed on both sides of the "
"paper, chapters start on right-hand pages, and right-hand pages have odd "
"numbers.  But in short reports, text often is printed only on one side of "
"the paper.  Also in short reports, chapters sometimes do not start on new "
"pages, but are printed on the same page as the end of the preceding chapter, "
"after a small amount of vertical whitespace."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22641
msgid ""
"You can use the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command with various arguments to "
"specify how chapters should be started in printed output and whether headers "
"should be formatted for printing on one or both sides of the paper "
"(single-sided or double-sided printing)."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22645
msgid ""
"Write the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command at the beginning of a line "
"followed by its argument.  For example, you would write the following to "
"cause each chapter to start on a fresh odd-numbered page:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22648
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@setchapternewpage odd\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22652
msgid ""
"You can specify one of three alternatives with the "
"@code{@@setchapternewpage} command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22655
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@setchapternewpage off"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22659
msgid ""
"Typeset a new chapter on the same page as the last chapter, after skipping "
"some vertical whitespace.  Also, format page headers for single-sided "
"printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22660
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@setchapternewpage on"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22664
msgid ""
"Start new chapters on new pages and format page headers for single-sided "
"printing.  This is the form most often used for short reports or personal "
"printing. This is the default."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22665
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@setchapternewpage odd"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22669
msgid ""
"Start new chapters on new, odd-numbered pages (right-handed pages)  and "
"typeset for double-sided printing.  This is the form most often used for "
"books and manuals."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22674
msgid ""
"Texinfo does not have a @code{@@setchapternewpage even} command, because "
"there is no printing tradition of starting chapters or books on an "
"even-numbered page."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22678
msgid ""
"If you don't like the default headers that @code{@@setchapternewpage} sets, "
"you can explicit control them with the @code{@@headings} command.  "
"@xref{@code{@@headings}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22683
msgid ""
"At the beginning of a manual or book, pages are not numbered---for example, "
"the title and copyright pages of a book are not numbered.  By convention, "
"table of contents and frontmatter pages are numbered with roman numerals and "
"not in sequence with the rest of the document."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22686
msgid ""
"The @code{@@setchapternewpage} has no effect in output formats that do not "
"have pages, such as Info and HTML."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22693
msgid ""
"We recommend not including any @code{@@setchapternewpage} command in your "
"document source at all, since such desired pagination is not intrinsic to "
"the document.  For a particular hard copy run, if you don't want the default "
"output (no blank pages, same headers on all pages) use the "
"@option{--texinfo} option to @command{texi2dvi} to specify the output you "
"want."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22695 texinfo/texinfo.texi:22699
#, no-wrap
msgid "Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22696
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page Headings"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22699
msgid "@nodedescription Page headings."
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22699
msgid "Headings Introduced"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22700
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footings"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22701
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page numbering"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22702
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22703
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting headings and footings"
msgstr ""

#.  HTML output also supports something like these, but in a
#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22711
msgid ""
"Most printed manuals contain headings along the top of every page except the "
"title and copyright pages.  Some manuals also contain footings. Headings and "
"footings have no meaning in Info or the other output formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22717
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides two standard heading formats, one for manuals printed on "
"one side of each sheet of paper, and the other for manuals printed on both "
"sides of the paper.  By default, nothing is specified for the footing of a "
"Texinfo file, so the footing remains blank."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22720
msgid ""
"Texinfo also has several heading and footing commands that you can use to "
"generate your own heading and footing formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22728
msgid ""
"In Texinfo, headings and footings are single lines at the tops and bottoms "
"of pages; you cannot create multiline headings or footings.  Each header or "
"footer line is divided into three parts: a left part, a middle part, and a "
"right part.  Any part, or a whole line, may be left blank.  Text for the "
"left part of a header or footer line is set flushleft; text for the middle "
"part is centered; and, text for the right part is set flushright."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22730
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@headings}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22733
msgid ""
"@nodedescriptionblock An option for turning headings on and off and double "
"or single sided printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22734
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @code{@@headings} Command"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22737
msgid "headings on off"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22737
#, no-wrap
msgid "headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22744
msgid ""
"The @code{@@headings} command is rarely used.  It specifies what kinds of "
"page headings and footings to print on each page.  Usually, this is "
"controlled by the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command.  You need the "
"@code{@@headings} command only if the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command "
"does not do what you want."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22746
msgid "You can use @code{@@headings} as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22748
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings off"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22750
msgid "Turn off printing of page headings."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22751
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings single"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22753
msgid "Turn on page headings appropriate for single-sided printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22754
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings double"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22756
msgid "Turn on page headings appropriate for double-sided printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22757
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings singleafter"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22758
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings doubleafter"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22761
msgid ""
"Turn on @code{single} or @code{double} headings, respectively, after the "
"current page is output."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22762
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings on"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22765
msgid ""
"Turn on page headings: @code{single} if @samp{@@setchapternewpage on}, "
"@code{double} otherwise."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22773
msgid ""
"For example, suppose you write @code{@@setchapternewpage off} before the "
"@code{@@titlepage} command to start a new chapter on the same page as the "
"end of the last chapter.  This command also causes page headers to be "
"typeset for single-sided printing.  To cause page headers to be typeset for "
"double-sided printing, write @code{@@headings double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22776
msgid ""
"You can stop any page headings at all from being generated by writing "
"@code{@@headings off} on a line of its own, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22779
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings off\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22782
#, no-wrap
msgid "Heading Format"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22784
msgid "@nodedescription Standard page heading formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22784
#, no-wrap
msgid "Standard Heading Formats"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22789
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides two standard heading formats, one for manuals printed on "
"one side of each sheet of paper, and the other for manuals printed on both "
"sides of the paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22794
msgid ""
"The standard format for single-sided printing consists of a header line in "
"which the left-hand part contains the name of the chapter, the central part "
"is blank, and the right-hand part contains the page number.  A single-sided "
"page looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22804
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  _______________________\n"
" |                       |\n"
" | chapter   page number |\n"
" |                       |\n"
" | Start of text ...     |\n"
" | ...                   |\n"
" |                       |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22815
msgid ""
"In the standard double-sided format, the left part of the left-hand "
"(even-numbered) page contains the page number, the central part is blank, "
"and the right part contains the title (specified by the @code{@@settitle} "
"command).  The left part of the right-hand (odd-numbered) page contains the "
"name of the chapter, the central part is blank, and the right part contains "
"the page number.  An even-numbered page and an odd-numbered page, side by "
"side as in an open book, look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22825
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  _______________________     _______________________\n"
" |                       |   |                       |\n"
" | page number     title |   | chapter   page number |\n"
" |                       |   |                       |\n"
" | Start of text ...     |   | More  text ...        |\n"
" | ...                   |   | ...                   |\n"
" |                       |   |                       |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22832
msgid ""
"The chapter name is preceded by the word ``Chapter'', the chapter number and "
"a colon.  This makes it easier to keep track of where you are in the manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22835
msgid ""
"Note that on pages where a new chapter starts, some text may be omitted from "
"the heading line."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22849
msgid ""
"By default, nothing is specified for the footing of a Texinfo file, so the "
"footing remains blank."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22852
#, no-wrap
msgid "Custom Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22854
msgid "@nodedescription How to create your own headings and footings."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22854
#, no-wrap
msgid "How to Make Your Own Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22859
msgid ""
"You can use the standard headings provided with Texinfo or specify your "
"own.  By default, Texinfo has no footers, so if you specify them, the "
"available page size for the main text will be slightly reduced."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22862
msgid "Texinfo provides six commands for specifying headings and footings:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22866
msgid ""
"@code{@@everyheading} and @code{@@everyfooting} generate page headers and "
"footers that are the same for both even- and odd-numbered pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22869
msgid ""
"@code{@@evenheading} and @code{@@evenfooting} commands generate headers and "
"footers for even-numbered (left-hand) pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22872
msgid ""
"@code{@@oddheading} and @code{@@oddfooting} generate headers and footers for "
"odd-numbered (right-hand) pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22876
msgid ""
"You must cancel the predefined heading commands with the @code{@@headings "
"off} command before defining your own specifications."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22881
msgid ""
"Here is how to place the chapter name at the left, the page number in the "
"center, and the date at the right of every header for both even- and "
"odd-numbered pages:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22886
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@headings off\n"
"@@everyheading @@thischapter @@| @@thispage @@| @@today@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22894
msgid ""
"You need to divide the left part from the central part and the central part "
"from the right part by inserting @samp{@@|} between parts.  Otherwise, the "
"specification command will not be able to tell where the text for one part "
"ends and the next part begins."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22899
msgid ""
"Each part can contain text or @@-commands.  The text is printed as if the "
"part were within an ordinary paragraph in the body of the page.  The "
"@@-commands replace themselves with the page number, date, chapter name, or "
"whatever."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22902
msgid "Here are the six heading and footing commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22904
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22905
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyfooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22906
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyheading @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22907
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyfooting @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22912
msgid ""
"The `every' commands specify the format for both even- and odd-numbered "
"pages.  These commands are for documents that are printed on one side of "
"each sheet of paper, or for documents in which you want symmetrical headers "
"or footers."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22913
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22914
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenfooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22915
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22916
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddfooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22917
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenheading @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22918
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddheading  @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22919
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenfooting @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22920
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddfooting  @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22924
msgid ""
"The `even' and `odd' commands specify the format for even-numbered pages and "
"odd-numbered pages.  These commands are for books and manuals that are "
"printed on both sides of each sheet of paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22931
msgid ""
"Use the @samp{@@this@dots{}} series of @@-commands to provide the names of "
"chapters and sections and the page number.  You can use the "
"@samp{@@this@dots{}} commands in the left, center, or right portions of "
"headers and footers."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22934
msgid "Here are the @samp{@@this@dots{}} commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22936
#, no-wrap
msgid "thispage"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22939
msgid "Expands to the current page number."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22940
#, no-wrap
msgid "thissectionname"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22943
msgid "Expands to the name of the current section."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22944
#, no-wrap
msgid "thissectionnum"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22947
msgid "Expands to the number of the current section."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22948
#, no-wrap
msgid "thissection"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22952
msgid ""
"Expands to the number and name of the current section, in the format "
"`Section 1: Title'."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22953
#, no-wrap
msgid "thischaptername"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22956
msgid "Expands to the name of the current chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22957
#, no-wrap
msgid "thischapternum"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22961
msgid ""
"Expands to the number of the current chapter, or letter of the current "
"appendix."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22962
#, no-wrap
msgid "thischapter"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22966
msgid ""
"Expands to the number and name of the current chapter, in the format "
"`Chapter 1: Title'."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22967
#, no-wrap
msgid "thistitle"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22971
msgid ""
"Expands to the name of the document, as specified by the @code{@@settitle} "
"command."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22972
#, no-wrap
msgid "thisfile"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22980
msgid ""
"For @code{@@include} files only: expands to the name of the current "
"@code{@@include} file.  If the current Texinfo source file is not an "
"@code{@@include} file, this command has no effect.  This command does "
"@emph{not} provide the name of the current Texinfo source file unless it is "
"an @code{@@include} file.  (@xref{Include Files}, for more information about "
"@code{@@include} files.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22985
msgid ""
"You can also use the @code{@@today@{@}} command, which expands to the "
"current date, in `1 Jan 1900' format."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22985
#, no-wrap
msgid "today"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22990
msgid ""
"Other @@-commands and text are printed in a header or footer just as if they "
"were in the body of a page.  It is useful to incorporate text, particularly "
"when you are writing drafts:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:22996
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@headings off\n"
"@@everyheading @@emph@{Draft!@} @@| @@thispage @@| @@thischapter\n"
"@@everyfooting @@| @@| Version: 0.27: @@today@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23001
msgid ""
"Beware of overlong titles: they may overlap another part of the header or "
"footer and blot it out."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23006
msgid ""
"If you have very short chapters and/or sections, several of them can appear "
"on a single page.  You can specify which chapters and sections you want "
"@code{@@thischapter}, @code{@@thissection} and other such macros to refer to "
"on such pages as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23008
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyheadingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23009
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyfootingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23010
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyheadingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23011
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyfootingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23017
msgid ""
"The @var{ref} argument can be either @code{top} (the @code{@@this...} "
"commands will refer to the chapter/section at the top of a page) or "
"@code{bottom} (the commands will reflect the situation at the bottom of a "
"page).  These @samp{@@every...} commands specify what to do on both even- "
"and odd-numbered pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23018
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenheadingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23019
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddheadingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23020
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenfootingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23021
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddfootingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23022
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenheadingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23023
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddheadingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23024
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenfootingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23025
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddfootingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23029
msgid ""
"These @samp{@@even...} and @samp{@@odd...} commands specify what to do on "
"only even- or odd-numbered pages, respectively.  The @var{ref} argument is "
"the same as with the @samp{@@every...} commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23035
msgid ""
"Write these commands immediately after the @code{@@...contents} commands, or "
"after the @code{@@end titlepage} command if you don't have a table of "
"contents or if it is printed at the end of your manual.  These commands have "
"no effect in @LaTeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23038
msgid ""
"By default, for @TeX{}, the @code{@@this...} commands reflect the situation "
"at the bottom of a page both in headings and in footings."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23040
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@paragraphindent}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23042
msgid "@nodedescription Specify paragraph indentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23042
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@paragraphindent}: Controlling Paragraph Indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23046
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indenting paragraphs, control of"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23047
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paragraph indentation control"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23054
msgid ""
"The Texinfo processors may insert whitespace at the beginning of the first "
"line of each paragraph, thereby indenting that paragraph.  You can use the "
"@code{@@paragraphindent} command to specify this indentation.  Write a "
"@code{@@paragraphindent} command at the beginning of a line followed by "
"either @samp{asis} or a number:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23057
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@paragraphindent @var{indent}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23060 texinfo/texinfo.texi:23150
msgid "The indentation is according to the value of @var{indent}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23065 texinfo/texinfo.texi:23155
msgid ""
"Do not change the existing indentation (not implemented in printed output)."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23067 texinfo/texinfo.texi:23156
#, no-wrap
msgid "0"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23069 texinfo/texinfo.texi:23158
msgid "Omit all indentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: var{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23070 texinfo/texinfo.texi:23159
#, no-wrap
msgid "n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23073
msgid ""
"Indent by @var{n} space characters in Info output, by @var{n} ems in printed "
"output."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23078
msgid ""
"The default value of @var{indent} is 3.  @code{@@paragraphindent} is ignored "
"for HTML output."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23083
msgid ""
"It is best to write the @code{@@paragraphindent} command before the "
"end-of-header line at the beginning of a Texinfo file, so the region "
"formatting commands indent paragraphs as specified.  @xref{Start of Header}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23085
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@firstparagraphindent}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23087
msgid "@nodedescription Suppressing first paragraph indentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23087
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@firstparagraphindent}: Indenting After Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23090
#, no-wrap
msgid "firstparagraphindent"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23091
#, no-wrap
msgid "First paragraph, suppressing indentation of"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23092
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppressing first paragraph indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23093
#, no-wrap
msgid "Preventing first paragraph indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23094
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indenting, suppressing of first paragraph"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23095
#, no-wrap
msgid "Headings, indentation after"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23102
msgid ""
"As you can see in the present manual, the first paragraph in any section is "
"not indented by default.  Typographically, indentation is a paragraph "
"separator, which means that it is unnecessary when a new section begins.  "
"This indentation is controlled with the @code{@@firstparagraphindent} "
"command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23105
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@firstparagraphindent @var{word}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23109
msgid ""
"The first paragraph after a heading is indented according to the value of "
"@var{word}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23115
msgid ""
"Prevents the first paragraph from being indented (default).  This option is "
"ignored by @command{makeinfo} if @code{@@paragraphindent asis} is in effect."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23116
#, no-wrap
msgid "insert"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23120
msgid ""
"Include normal paragraph indentation.  This respects the paragraph "
"indentation set by a @code{@@paragraphindent} command "
"(@pxref{@code{@@paragraphindent}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23123
msgid "@code{@@firstparagraphindent} is ignored for HTML and DocBook output."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23128
msgid ""
"It is best to write the @code{@@firstparagraphindent} command before the "
"end-of-header line at the beginning of a Texinfo file, so the region "
"formatting commands indent paragraphs as specified.  @xref{Start of Header}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23130
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@exampleindent}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23132
msgid "@nodedescription Specify environment indentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23132
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@exampleindent}: Environment Indenting"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23135
#, no-wrap
msgid "exampleindent"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23136
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indenting environments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23137
#, no-wrap
msgid "Environment indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23138
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23144
msgid ""
"The Texinfo processors indent each line of @code{@@example} and similar "
"environments.  You can use the @code{@@exampleindent} command to specify "
"this indentation.  Write an @code{@@exampleindent} command at the beginning "
"of a line followed by either @samp{asis} or a number:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23147
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@exampleindent @var{indent}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23162
msgid ""
"Indent environments by @var{n} space characters in Info output, by @var{n} "
"ems in printed output."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23168
msgid ""
"The default value of @var{indent} is 5 spaces in Info, and 0.4@dmn{in} in "
"printed output, which is somewhat less.  (The reduction is to help fit more "
"characters onto physical lines in printed manuals.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23173
msgid ""
"It is best to write the @code{@@exampleindent} command before the "
"end-of-header line at the beginning of a Texinfo file, so the region "
"formatting commands indent paragraphs as specified.  @xref{Start of Header}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23175
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@smallbook}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23177
msgid "@nodedescription How to print small format books and manuals."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23177
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@smallbook}: Printing ``Small'' Books"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23180
#, no-wrap
msgid "smallbook"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23181
#, no-wrap
msgid "Small book size"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23182
#, no-wrap
msgid "Book, printing small"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23183
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page sizes for books"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23184
#, no-wrap
msgid "Size of printed book"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23192
msgid ""
"By default, @TeX{} typesets pages for printing in an 8.5 by 11 inch format, "
"which is the ``letter'' size commonly used in the United States.  However, "
"you can direct @TeX{} or @LaTeX{} to typeset a document in a 7 by 9.25 inch "
"format that is suitable for bound books by inserting the following command "
"on a line by itself at the beginning of the Texinfo file, before the title "
"page:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23195
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@smallbook\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23202
msgid ""
"(Since many books are about 7 by 9.25 inches, this command might better have "
"been called the @code{@@regularbooksize} command, but it came to be called "
"the @code{@@smallbook} command by comparison to the 8.5 by 11 inch format.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23206
msgid ""
"@xref{Format with @command{texi2dvi} or @command{texi2pdf}}, and "
"@ref{Preparing for @TeX{}}, for other ways to format with @code{@@smallbook} "
"with @TeX{} that do not require changing the source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23208
#, no-wrap
msgid "A4 Paper"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23210
msgid "@nodedescription How to print on A4, A5 or B6 paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23210
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printing on A4 Paper"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23211
#, no-wrap
msgid "A4 paper, printing on"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23212
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paper size, A4"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23213
#, no-wrap
msgid "European A4 paper"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23214
#, no-wrap
msgid "afourpaper"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23218
msgid ""
"You can format a document for printing on ISO 216 A4 paper size with the "
"@code{@@afourpaper} command.  Write the command on a line by itself near the "
"beginning of the Texinfo file, before the title page."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23219
#, no-wrap
msgid "A5 paper, printing on"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23220
#, no-wrap
msgid "afourlatex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23221
#, no-wrap
msgid "afourwide"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23222
#, no-wrap
msgid "afivepaper"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23223
#, no-wrap
msgid "bsixpaper"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23228
msgid ""
"You may or may not prefer the formatting that results from the command "
"@code{@@afourlatex}.  There's also @code{@@afourwide} for A4 paper in wide "
"format, @code{@@afivepaper} for A5 paper, and @code{@@bsixpaper} for B6 "
"paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23232
msgid ""
"@xref{Format with @command{texi2dvi} or @command{texi2pdf}} and "
"@ref{Preparing for @TeX{}}, for other ways to format for different paper "
"sizes that do not require changing the source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23234
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@pagesizes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23236
msgid "@nodedescription How to print with customized page sizes."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23236
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@pagesizes} [@var{width}][, @var{height}]: Custom Page Sizes"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23238 texinfo/texinfo.texi:23239
#, no-wrap
msgid "pagesizes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23240
#, no-wrap
msgid "Custom page sizes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23241
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page sizes, customized"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23242
#, no-wrap
msgid "Text width and height"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23243
#, no-wrap
msgid "Width of text area"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23244
#, no-wrap
msgid "Height of text area"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23245
#, no-wrap
msgid "Depth of text area"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23252
msgid ""
"You can explicitly specify the height and (optionally) width of the main "
"text area on the page with the @code{@@pagesizes} command.  Write this on a "
"line by itself near the beginning of the Texinfo file, before the title "
"page.  The height comes first, then the width if desired, separated by a "
"comma.  Examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23255
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@pagesizes 200mm,150mm  @c for b5 paper\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23260
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@pagesizes 11.5in       @c for legal paper\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23262
#, no-wrap
msgid "B5 paper, printing on"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23263
#, no-wrap
msgid "Legal paper, printing on"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23268
msgid ""
"This would be reasonable for printing on B5-size paper.  To emphasize, this "
"command specifies the size of the @emph{text area}, not the size of the "
"paper (which is 250@dmn{mm} by 177@dmn{mm} for B5, 14@dmn{in} by 8.5@dmn{in} "
"for legal)."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23269
#, no-wrap
msgid "Margins on page, not controllable"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23273
msgid ""
"To make more elaborate changes, such as changing any of the page margins, "
"you must define a new command in @file{texinfo.tex} or @file{texinfo.cnf}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23277
msgid ""
"@xref{Format with @command{texi2dvi} or @command{texi2pdf}}, and "
"@ref{Preparing for @TeX{}}, for other ways to specify @code{@@pagesizes} "
"that do not require changing the source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23279 texinfo/texinfo.texi:23281
#, no-wrap
msgid "Microtypography"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23281
msgid "@nodedescription Improving the appearance of paragraphs."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23289
msgid ""
"Some versions of @TeX{}, namely pdf@TeX{} and Lua@TeX{}, have "
"@dfn{microtypography} features.  These involve stretching font glyphs "
"slightly, and allowing text to produce very slightly into the margins.  Use "
"of these gives @TeX{} more flexibility in breaking a paragraph into lines, "
"and can improve paragraph appearance by reducing hyphenation and producing a "
"more consistent typographic color."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23290
#, no-wrap
msgid "microtype"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23294
msgid ""
"@file{texinfo.tex} uses these features (`microtype' for short) when "
"available.  You can turn microtype off by specifying @samp{@@microtype off} "
"in your input file; likewise, @samp{@@microtype on} turns microtype back on."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23295
#, no-wrap
msgid "CM-Super fonts @subentry for @LaTeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23303
msgid ""
"The fonts used in @LaTeX{} output may not be scalable.  If fonts are not "
"scalable, using microtypography could trigger an error when converting the "
"@LaTeX{} output.  Therefore microtypography is not turned on in the default "
"case in @LaTeX{} output.  It is possible to turn it on with "
"@samp{@@microtype on}.  A way to get scalable fonts for the font used in the "
"default @LaTeX{} output is to install the CM-Super font package "
"(@url{http://ctan.org/pkg/cm-super})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23305
msgid "The @code{@@microtype} command does nothing for other output formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23307 texinfo/texinfo.texi:23309
#, no-wrap
msgid "Magnification"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23309
msgid "@nodedescription How to print scaled up output."
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23311
msgid "Cropmarks and Magnification"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23312
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\mag @r{(raw @TeX{} magnification)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23313
#, no-wrap
msgid "Magnified printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23314
#, no-wrap
msgid "Larger or smaller pages"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23322
msgid ""
"You can attempt to direct @TeX{} to typeset pages larger or smaller than "
"usual with the @code{\\mag} @TeX{} command.  Everything that is typeset is "
"scaled proportionally larger or smaller.  (@code{\\mag} stands for "
"``magnification''.)  This is @emph{not} a Texinfo @@-command, but is a raw "
"@TeX{} command that is prefixed with a backslash.  You have to write this "
"command between @code{@@tex} and @code{@@end tex} (@pxref{Raw Formatter "
"Commands})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23327
msgid ""
"Follow the @code{\\mag} command with an @samp{=} and then a number that is "
"1000 times the magnification you desire.  For example, to print pages at 1.2 "
"normal size, write the following near the beginning of the Texinfo file, "
"before the title page:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23333
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@tex\n"
"\\global\\mag=1200\n"
"@@end tex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23340
msgid ""
"With some printing technologies, you can print normal-sized copies that look "
"better than usual by giving a larger-than-normal master to your print shop.  "
"They do the reduction, thus effectively increasing the resolution."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23344
msgid ""
"Depending on your system, DVI files prepared with a nonstandard-@code{\\mag} "
"may not print or may print only with certain magnifications.  Be prepared to "
"experiment."
msgstr ""

#. type: appendix
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23346 texinfo/texinfo.texi:23348
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Specification"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23348
msgid "@nodedescription Technical details of the Info file format."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23350
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info format specification"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23351
#, no-wrap
msgid "Specification of Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23352
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition of Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23355
msgid "Here we describe the technical details of the Info format."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23364
msgid ""
"In this formal description, the characters @code{<>*()|=#} are used for the "
"language of the description itself.  Other characters are literal.  The "
"formal constructs used are typical: @code{<...>} indicates a metavariable "
"name, @samp{=} means definition, @samp{*} repetition, @samp{?} optional, "
"@samp{()} grouping, @samp{|} alternation, and @samp{#} comment."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23369
msgid ""
"In general, programs that read Info files should try to be case-insensitive "
"to keywords that occur in the file (for example, @samp{Tag Table} and "
"@samp{Tag table} should be equivalent) in order to support Info-generating "
"programs that use different capitalization."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23375
msgid ""
"We specify literal parentheses (those that are part of the Info format) with "
"@t{<lparen>} and @t{<rparen>}, meaning the single characters @samp{(} and "
"@samp{)} respectively.  Finally, the two-character sequence @samp{^@var{x}} "
"means the single character @samp{CTRL-@var{x}}, for any @var{x}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23383
msgid ""
"This format definition was written some 25 years after the Info format was "
"first devised.  So in the event of conflicts between this definition and "
"actual practice, practice wins.  It also assumes some general knowledge of "
"Texinfo; it is meant to be a guide for implementors rather than a rigid "
"technical standard.  We may refer back to other parts of this manual for "
"examples and definitions, rather than redundantly spelling out every detail."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23395
msgid "Whole: Info Format Whole Manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23395
msgid "Preamble: Info Format Preamble."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/texinfo.texi:23395
